WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.792
A test set your team can live with.

2
00:00:02.072 --> 00:00:03.757
Put it together into a routine.

3
00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:12.507
After each round of error analysis, add the confirmed failures as cases with expected behaviors, tagged by failure mode and source.

4
00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:20.403
Have the domain expert write cases for policies and edge cases that logs don’t show, and review a sample of everything else.

5
00:00:20.683 --> 00:00:28.341
Use synthetic generation along explicit dimensions to fill empty cells in the coverage matrix, and read what it produces.

6
00:00:28.621 --> 00:00:35.981
Keep golden, regression, and development pools separate, version them in the repo, and record the set version with every result.

7
00:00:36.261 --> 00:00:38.267
And keep the n next to every number.

8
00:00:38.547 --> 00:00:42.045
This also sets up model selection for the rest of the program.

9
00:00:42.325 --> 00:00:54.485
A versioned golden set built from your own traffic is what lets you compare a Claude model against a GPT, Gemini, Grok, or open-weight model on your work, instead of on someone else’s benchmark.

10
00:00:54.765 --> 00:01:03.981
Next week you’ll build the graders that score it: code assertions first, then LLM judges, calibrated against the expert’s labels from this week.
